{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that centralized coordination enables synchronized global rate-limiting and load shedding to prevent cascading failures, but the problem specifically concerns CORRELATED failure, and its own countercondition concedes the orchestrator itself can be a source of correlated crashes. The evidence-distinguishing requirement of the prompt is entirely unmet: SRC_001 never identifies evidence that would separate genuinely independent failures from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. Its own SPOF countercondition is exactly a shared-cause mechanism it fails to operationalize a test for, leaving no way to distinguish apparent independence from hidden correlation.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism depends on all decentralized nodes adhering to one common communication protocol, so a shared protocol bug becomes a correlated trigger. But it explicitly omits the symmetric case from its analysis: the large redundant service also depends on internal protocols, and unless those protocols are different and independently implemented, the same correlated protocol failure can crash every replica at once under identical inputs. Its second countercondition acknowledges this equality ('making it equally susceptible'), which concedes the fault it tries to deny for the large service. It therefore cannot support the comparative resilience claim it asserts for the large service.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 asserts rapid synchronized patching as a centralized advantage, but the problem asks for evidence to distinguish truly independent failure modes from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. SRC_003 never supplies such evidence; instead its own countercondition concedes internal redundancy implemented as exact replicas is susceptible to identical-input crashes, a paradigmatic hidden shared cause that makes failures appear independent until the same input or bug triggers them together. The central monitoring it invokes cannot detect this until the correlated trigger hits, so it cannot meet the distinguishing-evidence requirement.","counterexample":null}]}